## Summary

Wiggot is a real estate platform for agents. It had duplicated interfaces, an undocumented design system and a fragmented mobile experience. With two other designers I created a new design system on top of Material Design using Atomic Design, and redesigned search, the listings exchange, shared lists and the property card. Agents found the platform clearer and less cluttered on desktop and mobile.

{% figure
   src="/images/projects/wiggot/new-ui.png"
   alt="New Wiggot screens on desktop and mobile"
   caption="The new system applied to lists, listings and profile."
   wide=false /%}

## The problem

The product had several designs for the same feature, information without a clear hierarchy, and key sections that didn't adapt to mobile. The existing design system had no documentation, so every change was more expensive and the experience felt fragmented.

{% figure
   src="/images/projects/wiggot/system-map.png"
   alt="Map of the previous, undocumented design system"
   caption="The previous system: loose, undocumented pieces."
   wide=true /%}

## My role

UX strategy, wireframes and high-fidelity prototypes in Figma, usability testing with internal users, and system documentation so engineering could scale it. I also took part in research, benchmarking, data synthesis, co-ideation sessions and user flows.

## What we found

- **Not built for mobile:** several key sections weren't optimized for small screens.
- **Too many interfaces:** there were multiple versions of similar features, which confused users.
- **Weak information architecture:** information didn't follow a clear, logical flow.
- **Competitors** had a consistent system that reinforced their brand and efficiency.

{% figure
   src="/images/projects/wiggot/research.png"
   alt="Research synthesis and wireframes"
   caption="Aligning features and responsive versions."
   wide=false /%}

## Key decisions

1. **Material Design as the base** instead of a system built from scratch, to gain speed and parity between desktop and mobile.
1. **Atomic Design for documentation:** atoms, molecules, organisms, templates and pages. Engineering finds every piece by its level.
1. **A mandatory documentation process:** build the component, document it in the template and announce it in the team channel.

